node.doctext:
If I understood your question correctly, you are trying to get two inputs from user in terminal say cat, dog and if those columns values in the table are matching with cat and dog respectively, <br>you need to select some other columns in the matching row and print it out.<br> If that is what you are looking for here you go, Untested though!

<code>
#!/usr/bin/perl -w
use strict;
use warnings;
use DBI;

#Get first input
print "Enter cpt code> ";
my $cptcode = <STDIN>;

#Get Second input
print "Enter cpt2 code> ";
my $cpt2code = <STDIN>;

if ( ($cptcode != “”) && ($cpt2code != “”)) {
        my $dbh = DBI->connect('dbi:mysql:mydatabase:localhost:3306','
+abc','xyz') or die "Couldn't connect to database: " . DBI->errstr;
        my $sth = $dbh->prepare('SELECT * FROM charge WHERE column1 = $cptcode AND column2 = $cpt2code’)
                or die "Couldn't prepare statement: " . $dbh->errstr;

                 $sth->execute()    # Execute the query
                or die "Couldn't execute statement: " . $sth->errstr;

        # Read the matching records and print them out
        while (my @data = $sth->fetchrow_array()) {
                my $cptanswer = $data[1];
                my $insid = $data[2];
                my $preauth = $data[3];
                my $updated = $data[5];
                print "\t$cptanswer\t$insid\t$preauth\t$updated\n";
        }

        if ($sth->rows == 0) {
                print "No names matched `$cptcode'.\n\n";
        }

        $sth->finish; 

        $dbh->disconnect;
}
</code>
